[{:seon.task/name "main-wins-a-conflict"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: when an agent merges main (`seon.agent/merge-main!`) and both sides changed a key, `seon.merge/three-way` names the conflict, the branch keeps its own content, and the conflict stays an unmerged entry (`seon.merge/unmerged-tx`) until the branch redefines the key or resolves it (`seon.merge/resolve-tx`); docs/parity.md: ours is kept. v1 (MERGE-STEP 5a): main's content wins on the branch and the branch's own is kept on the entry (:seon.merge/kept), so the agent runs main's definition and re-applies its change knowingly. Correct: merge-main! applies theirs for a conflict too, the entry holds :seon.merge/kept, and `seon.merge/unmerged` keeps it open until the branch resolves it. The attribute's name waits on the owner's D6 ruling, shared with seon.merge/a-merge-task-carries-what-it-names."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-conflict-on-merging-main-runs-mains-definition-and-keeps-ours}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-task-carries-what-it-names"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: a merge task merges main into the work branch whole (`seon.agent/merge-main!`) and the writer merges the whole branch, scratch helpers included. Owner, 2026-10-22: nothing moves between branches by default. Correct (DESIGN-tasks §3, prototyped: triple conflict, triples copy, nope missing, no scratch carried): :seon.task/carry ([:var sym] [:schema k] [:datum e a]), :seon.task/source, :seon.task/target; the merge branch starts at the target's head; each item judged against the merge base as missing, same, copy or conflict; a carried function's branch-only callees not carried are missing (v1: a reach-chosen list missed 4 chain-only helpers); a conflict entry records the side the branch holds (:seon.merge/kept) so `seon.merge/unmerged` stays open until resolved. Deletes: the merge task's whole-branch merge-main."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/a-merge-task-judges-each-item seon.merge-test/an-uncarried-branch-only-callee-is-missing}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-outside-branch-hands-back-through-the-store" :seon.task/plan "Whether a Claude worktree branch can merge through the store (the design in docs/merging.md; the review docs/merging.md says: on hold until experiment 3's diff is empty). Measured (the scratch paper merge-probe-2026-10-27.md (git history); probe root on 4840ddb's first parent, jolt/data-attrs-from-the-index imported by `seon.source/refresh-tx`): experiment 1 holds on correctness: :ours of `seon.merge/delta-three-way` is exactly the definitions git's diff touches (10 vars, 16 forms), 0 conflicts, 67 datoms written for the branch, `seon.source/files` byte-equal to git show tip for both files; but it cost 12,607 calls and 277 MB, not about 39 and under 600 KB (main's side changed 79 keys since the base, and the values were d/db-with copies, not store branches: the number on real d/branch! branches is not measured). Experiment 2 fails: the merged program's 917 tests all plan to run, 0 reused from test-results/ that bin/prove --merge proved green; the first differing digest is datahike.query-test/a-nested-call-in-a-predicate-is-refused (import 11baa001 against 5 stored greens). Cause, partial: every digest takes :env, which digests `seon.fx/config-input`, the whole config in force less :seon.config/place keys; the probe root's config was not bin/test's (a --set of agent max-running, its own --config layer); restoring that key changed the digest and still left 917. Next probe: compare bin/test --plan's per-test digests on 4840ddb's tree with the import's, one by one. Experiment 3, not run: on a worktree root (its own store, never runs/store, :seon.config.source/branch set to a probe-only branch so write-back cannot touch jolt/merges or jolt/seon-merges), attach a task, hand back a past branch that touched src, test, default.edn and a bin script and deleted something, through seon_attach and seon_done; measure whether the judge ran on the imported code (the review: the agent's hand-back runs the proofs on its loaded program, so the import must be the agent's own, as ``seon.agent/load-tests!`` (since deleted) does, before hand-back), what the verdict tree held against git show tip (default.edn, bin and deps.edn are not in the store), and the write-back commit's tree diff against the branch tip, file modes included (`seon.fx/git-commit` writes every file 100644 and cannot delete). Routing Claude worktrees through the store waits until that diff is empty." :seon.task/kind :work :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/an-outside-branch-writes-back-its-own-tree}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-merge-system"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: which runner (seon.prove or bin/prove) is the one verdict, and whether a synchronous seon_done may run process tests on every merge (it overturns the 2026-10-23 process-tests ruling), are undecided and are the owner's; experiment 3 of seon.merge/an-outside-branch-hands-back-through-the-store has not run. The open design (plan direction 3; this task holds docs/merging.md and its review docs/merging.md): one merge system from what Seon already has, not two. Facts (main 4840ddb153): since 2026-10-20 main took 301 first-parent merges, all by git (worktree merges and bin/prove --merge); Seon's own merge (octopus plus write-back) made 14 Seon merge commits, all only on jolt/seon-merges (the review: 0 in main). Duplications the audit named: two mains (git and the store, synced by seon.source/refresh-tx on watch and by write-back plus bin/update); two verdicts (the judge defers process tests and the reconciler's :gate runs them; bin/prove --merge runs every key with no green) and a third runner, seon.prove, the test service (the review: the design never says what becomes of it, and the :tests effect would need a new contract and a lock); two serializers (bin/env's flock and the reconciler's one merge flight); two records (facts and the merge log, now the merge commit message); a merge task per work task (done: a work task merges itself through the one gate, docs/merging.md); three branch-vs-base diffs (seon.merge/delta-changes, seon.program/divergence, seon.program/diff-plan, the last for loading). Owner rulings that bound it: Seon's merge path stays whole and is exercised before anything is cut (do not throw out the baby); a work task merges itself; worktrees retire. Review corrections to apply to the design before anything is built: octopus and merge-keys are not dead under max-running 1 (that caps only :merge-kind starts; whole tasks in one judge pass merge as one octopus), so cutting to one branch is a behaviour change; gate-checkout is set by no local.edn (the gate has never run on the standing root); the branch-key mismatch is worktree roots writing the shared ref (done: bin/env names jolt/seon-merges-<worktree>); the import must be the agent's own before hand-back (seon.agent/load-tests!), not root's after it; the verdict tree must be the branch's own tree because default.edn, system.edn, tasks, bin and deps.edn are not in the store; seon.fx/git-commit writes mode 100644 and cannot delete (a bin script loses its executable bit, deletions are routine), so pin bumps, native code and deletions stay git merges until it can; a Seon merge commit with no branch parent makes the branch look unmerged and drops its measured commit message; a synchronous seon_done running process tests on every merge overturns the 2026-10-23 process-tests ruling and needs the owner. Order: the deletions that stand alone first (done: file tasks merge themselves; the gate and :unrun problem and the merge-log as a render of facts remain); routing worktrees through the store waits on seon.merge/an-outside-branch-hands-back-through-the-store (experiment 3). Correct: the design names which runner (seon.prove or bin/prove) is the one verdict and deletes the other's verdict role, and carries the two experiments' numbers. Proof to write: after the build, no second verdict path is left (a resolve of the gate's vars is nil) and one result store answers both bin/test and the judge (0 keys to run for a tree bin/prove --merge proved)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.merge/an-outside-branch-hands-back-through-the-store"}}
 {:seon.task/name "what-merges-is-what-a-file-states"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-30: \"it should be the same sync system\"; \"we do need a one way overwrite on things. Is the wiki that different? Don't overthink this if there's a clear solution\"; \"we need to be clear in the diffs that agents are getting when we are merging from main and something is changing\". (A :seon.merge/carried kind on entity schemas was built and dropped: \"the carried type is a kind and you aren't supposed to do that\".) Found on md-scan 29: main moved its wiki under a living branch, merge-main carried the code and not the sections, and seon.problems/caused-split held its done on a catch main's wiki named.\n\nBuilt (3a69494970): a section is a program key, [:section address], in the one three-way beside [:schema k] (docs/merging.md#seons-merge-is-a-three-way-merge-of-the-program): main's change to a section the branch did not change overwrites it, one both changed is an unmerged entry, main's new and removed pages reach the branch; a branch's own section change is refused by the writer as :seon.merge/unwritten until pages are written back.\n\nLeft, in order: (1) an agent is told what came in: seon.turn/work!'s first merge-main! discards its answer today, and the merge's applied and conflicts reach no context; the agent's next request carries what main brought, grouped by kind (functions, schemas, wiki sections, task content), each a count and its names, and the form that reads one change as clojure.data/diff of base and main, a conflict with base, ours and theirs and the form that resolves it, drawn by the catch-up's declared model render, sent once; proof seon.agent-test/an-agent-is-told-what-came-in-from-main. (2) write-back: the writer writes a changed section back to its page and a changed task entry back to its task file, once each round-trips exactly (file, entities, file), and :seon.merge/unwritten goes; then seon.task/carry-tx folds into the three-way (task content a key like a section). (3) the data-attrs argument (root api :all, my.branch and the writer #{}) and seon.merge/carried-platform-attrs and platform-record? deleted: one merge carries one thing whoever asks.\n\nFor the owner, 2026-10-31 (the sessions' proposal, not yet approved): an agent cannot change the wiki at all today, which the owner's direction (agents improve the wiki through what they cite) needs. Found by the fence bench (bench-fence-1, 0 of 2): asked to make \"fence\" a library word in AGENTS.md section 7a, the agent asked at turn 7 whether AGENTS.md was tracked so it could edit it, nothing said how, and it looped 25 turns. What is missing, in order, each reusing what exists: (a) one documented page write for an agent, e.g. my.doc/write! of an address or path to its text, that loads it as seon.doc/load-tx does into the agent's branch (the section entities the three-way already merges; no file is touched), with a help-page section saying how; (b) write-back, step (2) above: the merges branch's :git effect writes each changed page back to docs/ as forms are written back to src/, round-tripping exactly; (c) the writer's :seon.merge/unwritten refusal goes once (b) holds; (d) code and tests that read the wiki read it from the branch's db, not the checkout's files, so an agent's change is judged where it was made. Then a wiki-edit bench task (the fence task, parked until (a)-(d)). Checked 2026-11-06: step (2) is built for pages: f39b5c3521 writes a merge's changed doc pages back (seon.source/changed-files, seon.doc/files, my.root/write-back!) and the :seon.merge/unwritten refusal is gone from src (proved by seon.merge-test/a-branch-doc-edit-merges-and-its-page-is-written-back, green; seon.merge-test/a-branch-takes-mains-wiki is green). Not built: step (1) (seon.agent-test/an-agent-is-told-what-came-in-from-main is not written), task entries written back to their files, step (3) (seon.merge/carried-platform-attrs and platform-record? exist), and my.doc/write! (src/my/doc.clj holds only reviewed!)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/a-branch-takes-mains-wiki seon.merge-test/a-branch-doc-edit-merges-and-its-page-is-written-back}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-takes-options"
  :seon.task/plan "Approved by the owner, 2026-11-08, with seon.merge/what-a-side-never-held-is-no-change, as the user-branch merges. Today the merge picks keys and conflict handling inside its callers. Correct: one function of target, source and base, each in `:seon.db/at-opts`' shape, never a db value, and options: which keys, `-X theirs` or `-X ours`, which data. A cherry-pick records no second parent and doesn't move the base. Proof, as in the lab: send one namespace of two to main, the other stays on the user's branch, a pull back loses nothing, and a whole merge later brings the rest; with the same var changed on both sides, the default leaves one unmerged entry, `-X theirs` leaves none and main's source. Out of scope: who calls it. See docs/users.md#the-merges."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/a-cherry-pick-leaves-the-rest-to-merge-later seon.merge-test/x-theirs-takes-the-upstreams-value}}
 {:seon.task/name "what-a-side-never-held-is-no-change"
  :seon.task/plan "Approved by the owner, 2026-11-08, with seon.merge/a-merge-takes-options. Found 2026-11-07 on a scratch store through the real seon.merge (docs/sharing.md#where-a-fact-lives): s2 takes a program-only merge from U with U as a parent, so its merge base holds a datom D it never held; D then changes on U; s2's whole merge reports [:datum e a] :deleted-by-them, though s2 never had or deleted it. No data changes, but a person or agent is asked to settle nothing. Correct: a key the side lacks with no retraction in its history is no change of that side, as seon.merge/carried-tx already reads retractions. Proof, red first: that sequence reports no conflict; a real retraction on a session against a change on U still does. Needed when user branches take whole merges: no path today merges whole in that order."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/side-never-held-datom-d-base-holds-d}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-session-syncs-with-main-unless-private"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: :seon.agent/private exists only on the unlanded jolt/sync (2026-11-09); ready only when that branch lands or the owner retires it. Owner, 2026-11-09: \"I want agents to be able to get any data from any previous sessions ... the ability to specify if agent sessions should be 'private' (not auto syncing with main) or always syncing everything\"; ruled: \"The private session only takes from main what is on main at the time it is branched.\" Spec by Web (worked with Context and Optimization). What exists, read on main: an agent's own pulls of main pass data-attrs #{} (my.branch/merge-main!, seon.turn at a turn whose main moved), seon.root/request defaults :all (two defaults for one operation); merges up (the gate's :merge-candidate, the reconciler's octopus merge) pass #{}; and seon.merge/platform-record? keeps every seon.* attribute but carried-platform-attrs out of every merge, so an agent's evals, model calls, cites, searches and delivered messages never reach main, and main's status, model, max-turns and messages never reach a branch by merge. Nothing on main reads an agent's evals or model calls (measured 2026-11-09: the judge and problems read the task's cites; cross-agent reads go through the union).\n\nThe change:\n- One setting, data on the agent entity: ``:seon.agent/private true`` (absent: syncing; no kind).\n- Private: the branch is main as it was at the fork, and takes nothing from main after (program included); its data never goes up.\n- Syncing (the default): down, everything on main that does not conflict, data included, at each turn's open where :seon.agent/main-commit is written; up, everything it made that does not conflict, its work included, with conflicts kept as unmerged entries the agent resolves (seon.merge/unmerged-tx), as program conflicts are.\n- One down-merge default for every caller, taken from the setting; seon.root/request's own :all goes.\n- What may cross is decided by writer, not namespace: what root writes on main about an agent (status, process, boot, model, max-turns) is main's half and goes down only; what the agent writes on its branch is its work. carried-platform-attrs becomes a case of the rule. Content-id entities (results, messages, cites) never conflict.\n- Sharing: content-addressed nodes (docs/sharing.md#what-a-branchs-nodes-are) keep a syncing branch mostly main's nodes.\nMeasured 2026-11-09 (one run each, load 3-5): a merge is flat in main's size (main 7.6x, cost +3-6%); carrying data (:all) costs about 60k ticks a datom moved (merge-tx 7.5k, seon.merge/delta-three-way under :all about 44k: a cost to cut first), 3.0M ticks at 10 datoms, 62M and 153 MB at 1,000; program only 1.8-6.8M; one agent run wrote 432 datoms in 33 transactions (about 65 a turn), so an up-merge a turn is about 7M ticks at today's cost.\n\nSettled with the owner, 2026-11-09 (\"I do want options for agents to be private (experimental not syncing with main the entire time) and an auto sync feature (likely the default behavior) which is always trying to sync everything that we can except for the conflicts which they have to resolve\"; \"I like the idea of only syncing data that's not invalid. If we aren't testing something and there's no conflict I guess it just goes through\"; \"an agent can modify their own view, but they can't modify the whole system without an explicit save\"):\n- Syncing goes up at each turn's end, what it made since its last sync, less what is held: a name with an open unmerged entry, and a change a reached test holds red (the tests the gate runs today, seon.reconcile's done judgment; the write check already refuses an invalid value). A change no test reaches and no conflict touches goes through. What is held stays on the branch and goes up at the first sync after it is resolved or green.\n- Private goes up only when the person saves it: the save is a fact on the agent (desired state, as every request to the reconciler), and the reconciler merges the branch, held to the same rule, retrying as what held it changes; a save is also how a private session becomes a syncing one. Today's automatic merge of a green task goes: a private branch reaches main only by a save, a syncing one by its syncs.\n- Who sets private: the person, per session.\n- What holds a change is data the agent reads on its page: each unmerged entry (base, ours, theirs) and each hold reason (the red test and its first failure) is an entity, the entry with a ref to its branch (Optimization's re-model 3), each drawn by its schema's render in the agent's page parts, so the model reads it in each turn's diff and the person sees the same (Web writes the two renders). Today's main line on an outside agent's eval (seon.agent/main-line) becomes a reading of the same entities.\n\nProofs, red first: a syncing session's eval, cite and search reach main, and a session started after reads them; a private session takes nothing from main after its fork and nothing of it reaches main; root's half of an agent never merges up; a data conflict is an unmerged entry the agent resolves; merge-main! asked through root's api and from a turn carry the same data for one session. Cost proofs: a turn's up-merge costs the datoms it carries; delta-three-way under :all costs the datoms that differ, near merge-tx's 7.5k a datom.\n\nState: being built by Optimization on jolt/sync, not landed; what was built when is in that branch's log (history is git's)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/syncing-session-s-eval-cite-search-reach-main}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-data-conflict-holds-the-merge-up"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed 2026-11-11 (merge safety inventory). Reproduction: main and a branch each change one task's :task/plan to its own value; `seon.agent/merge-main!` (data :all) names the conflict [:datum e :task/plan] and writes its unmerged entry, but `seon.merge/unmerged` reads the branch's content at the entry's key with `seon.merge/program-of` and no data attributes (#{}), so a datum's content reads nil, differs from :ours, and the entry reads as resolved; the writer's `seon.writer/merge-refused` then lets the branch up and main's value is replaced by the branch's (measured: main's plan became the branch's). The same path carries a branch's change onto an entity main retracted whole (conflict 3, a retraction against a change: main ends holding a note with only its text). What allowed it: an entry does not remember the data attributes its merge read with. Correct: the entry's key is read with the data attributes that made it (a datum key is data by its own shape), so an unmerged datum holds the merge up until resolved; the sweep: every reader of `seon.merge/unmerged` and `seon.merge/entries`. Owner: Optimization (seon.merge)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-safety-test/a-data-conflict-holds-the-merge-up-broken seon.merge-safety-test/a-retraction-against-a-change-is-a-conflict-broken}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-identity-on-two-entities-is-a-conflict"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed 2026-11-11 (merge safety inventory). Reproduction: main and a branch each make an entity with one value of a :db.unique/identity attribute that is no content id (a chosen name, :task/name; or a ref, :seon.vector/of, the data model session's four clashes on a real branch). `seon.merge/three-way` sees two keys, [:datum e1 a] and [:datum e2 a], and no conflict; the merge of main into the branch (`seon.merge/merge-tx` over ``datahike.api/merge-db``) throws :transact/unique from ``datahike.db.transaction/validate-datom-upsert``, so the merge is an exception, never an unmerged entry (measured). Inside one transaction a map naming the second entity throws :transact/upsert (``datahike.db.transaction/check-upsert-conflict``), and a map naming none upserts onto main's entity. Today :seon.vector/of is a platform record, so Seon's merge never carries it and the clash stays latent until a merge of everything. Correct: the three-way reads, for each identity value a side asserted, whether the other side holds it on another entity, and answers the conflict as an unmerged entry naming both entities; the data model session's alternative removes the class for derived data (its identity becomes its inputs' content id). Owner: Optimization with Data model."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-safety-test/two-entities-claiming-one-identity-are-a-conflict-broken seon.merge-safety-test/one-ref-identity-on-two-entities-is-a-conflict-broken}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-write-to-an-entity-the-other-side-retracted-is-a-conflict"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed 2026-11-11 (merge safety inventory). Reproduction: main retracts a task whole, the branch gives it a ref. Two keys, no conflict; `seon.merge/merge-tx` retracts the entity whole on the branch because main retracted its identity (its `gone`), so the branch's own write is dropped with no unmerged entry (measured). The other direction (main extends, the branch retracts) leaves the branch holding a task with only a ref after merging main (see seon.db/a-merge-passes-the-write-check). Correct (conflict 3 of the data model session's definition): an entity one side retracted whole and the other side wrote since the base is one conflict, an unmerged entry naming the entity and both sides' datoms. Owner: Optimization."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-safety-test/a-branchs-write-to-an-entity-main-retracted-is-kept-or-a-conflict-broken}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-carried-ref-names-an-entity-the-target-holds"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed 2026-11-11 (merge safety inventory). Reproduction: the branch writes {:db/id task :task/refs [{:var/name 'gen.only/v}]}: the var is a content id with no form, so no program key and not carried, while the datum [:datum task :task/refs v] is; main then holds a ref to an entity with no datoms (measured). ``datahike.db.utils/entid-strict`` takes any number as an entity. Correct: `seon.merge/carried-tx` carries, with a datum whose value is a ref, the datoms of the entity it names that the target lacks (as it does a form's namespace), so a merge of everything carries what it refers to; the sweep: every ref attribute a datum key can hold. Owner: Optimization."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-safety-test/a-carried-ref-names-an-entity-main-holds-broken}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-value-compares-by-content"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed 2026-11-11 (merge safety inventory). Measured on Jolt: (= (byte-array [1 2]) (byte-array [1 2])) is false, so `seon.merge/same?` (=) reads the same bytes written on both sides as a conflict, and every bytes datum read on two sides as changed. Datahike compares values by content in the index and, since Datahike 1edda667 (jolt/txsafe, 2026-11-11), in a transaction's same-value, cas, tuple and content-id checks too, through ``datahike.datom/value=`` (`=`, or ``datahike.datom/compare-value`` equal): measured, the proof's \"writing the bytes an entity holds is a no-op\" now holds; its two other assertions are Seon's and Jolt's. Correct: the merge compares values as the index does, with ``datahike.datom/value=`` (one compare, by content, for every value type the schema admits); the sweep: every = over datom values in src/seon (changes, three-way, unmerged). Owner: Optimization."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-safety-test/byte-values-compare-by-content-broken}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-replays-the-sources-net-change"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Deep fixes builds it (owner's ruling, 2026-11-11), Optimization reviews every line, Data model makes the schema commit (deletions only). The ruling it is held to: docs/decisions.md, 'A merge replays the source's transactions on the branch that receives it' and 'No derived value is stored' (2026-11-11); this entry adds only what the three sessions settled after it and what the build deletes. What exists: ```seon.merge/carried-tx``` copies raw datoms with their own transactions and ```seon.merge/merge-tx``` picks what crosses from hand-kept lists (```seon.merge/carried-platform-attrs```); main takes branches through the writer's octopus merge (src/seon/writer.clj) and an agent takes main through ```seon.agent/merge-main!```; conflicts are written as facts (:seon.merge/*, :seon.task/conflicts, :seon.task/unmerged, :seon.task/task-conflicts). Correct, one function for both directions: (merge! conn source opts) answers {:merged n :commit id}, or {:conflicts [{:key [identity-attr identity-value attribute] :base :ours :theirs}] :base-commit :source-commit} having written NOTHING. (1) Each side's net change since the merge base, from Datahike's diff of the base commit against each head. (2) A conflict is one key both sides changed to different values, by the entity's identity attribute, never its entity id. For an attribute of cardinality one the key is [identity-attr identity-value attribute]; for cardinality many both sides adding different members is NO conflict (they merge, two datoms), and a conflict is one MEMBER one side asserted or kept and the other retracted, its key [identity-attr identity-value attribute value] (today a task's proofs changed to two different sets conflict whole; per member is the owner's 'all non-conflicting', and it changes that case: decided here, Data model's ruling 2026-11-11). A ref's :base :ours :theirs are the target's identity pair, never an id. An entity in the net change with NO identity attribute is refused, naming its schema: a thing that crosses branches has an identity, and the merge never falls back to an entity id. The cases are their own entries: seon.merge/a-data-conflict-holds-the-merge-up, seon.merge/one-identity-on-two-entities-is-a-conflict, seon.merge/a-write-to-an-entity-the-other-side-retracted-is-a-conflict, seon.merge/a-carried-ref-names-an-entity-the-target-holds, and a retraction against a change; a schema that differs in kind is a conflict. Conflicts are COMPUTED by this one function, for the page and for the writer's refusal, and nothing of one is stored. (3) No conflict: the source's TRANSACTIONS since the base, each whole (all it wrote, the values a later transaction replaced included), in source order, each as ops (never raw datoms) in its own new transaction of the receiving branch. Not the net change cut by transaction: built and refused 2026-11-11 (a value written twice leaves its first transaction only the retraction of the base value, and the write check refuses it, 'requires :task/plan'; branch jolt/mergefn), so every state the receiving branch passes through is one the source held. Each goes through ``datahike.writer/apply!``, the last with the merge's parents and {:op 'merge!}, then ``datahike.writer/commit-pending!``; any refusal is ``datahike.writer/abandon-pending!``, so it is all or none and never shares its batch with other queued writes. Each replayed transaction carries the source's :seon.tx/branch; no new attribute. A fact asserted and retracted inside the source's change is written and retracted again, as the source did; a no-history retraction rides the last replayed transaction. The conflict check of (2) still reads the NET change: a conflict is about ends. (4) Main's side runs inside the writer's proc as its own op, the conflict check on both sides' net change inside that one op, so two agents merging at once are judged one after the other. (5) The agent's side takes resolutions {key value} as an argument, and only on a merge DOWN (main into its branch); a merge up refuses with the conflicts. It deletes: seon.merge's carried-tx, merge-tx and the lists and predicates that pick what crosses, the conflict facts and their attributes (:seon.merge/*, :seon.task/conflicts, :seon.task/unmerged, :seon.task/task-conflicts), the two keys of a task's stored evidence that hold a past run's conflicts (:conflicts and :unmerged of :seon.task/evidence: a past run's conflicts are computed from its base and source commits; evidence keeps :red and :from), and the octopus merge's :data-attrs. It does NOT delete ```seon.task/carry-tx```: that is no merge but the one named exception, the task content a run's END carries to main also when its branch does not merge (docs/tasks.md#task-content-reaches-main-at-the-runs-end; a refine edits the plan on the work's branch and only the plan reaches main); its set (today seon.merge/carried-platform-attrs, read in seon.task and seon.agent) moves to seon.task, named for what it is. Every reader goes in the same tip, counted on main cc223ac328 (grep over src/seon outside merge.clj): carried-platform-attrs in seon.agent (1) and seon.task (2), which read the set that moves to seon.task; merge-tx in seon.agent (1), seon.program (1) and seon.writer (4); :data-attrs in seon.agent (1), seon.reconcile (1), seon.root (5) and seon.writer (3); the tip's audit shows each count at 0. Cost line: n = the datoms a turn wrote (about 400 measured), m = what main wrote since the base; reads O(n + m), writes every datom the source wrote (n, not its net) in as many transactions as the source made; reading a transaction's datoms is today a diff of the two history views, which reads the range and not the difference: its cost on a real turn is measured before the writer's op uses it, and if it follows the history's size the diff of the history indexes goes into the Datahike fork; expected the order of today's octopus merge of a turn; measured before the writer's op is changed, on real turns of the standing system. Pieces: this entry; then the pure function of (2), which nothing calls until the writer's op; then (3) and (4) with the deletions in the same tip; then (5). Proofs: test/seon/merge_safety_test.clj as it stands on branch jolt/onemerge2 (414 lines: any valid branch merges, main is valid after, a pull of each merged entity equals its pull on the branch, each conflict case refused with nothing written), rewritten where it assumed one transaction a merge."
